{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"KEYSTONE DECISION — how a workflow step names a CLASS. Recorded on the parent because children 172e\nand 7761 BOTH block on it (10 blocking findings between them, one root cause), and cc33 inherits it.\n\nTHE GAP the reviews found: f844 shipped the class system, but NOTHING can consume it from a workflow.\nThe v3 step schema is closed/immutable and offers only 'model:' (a literal id string) and\n'model_ladder:' (a list of literal strings). There is no 'class:' key, and the reviewers correctly\nrefused plans that said a step should 'express the per-pass class' without saying how.\n\nDECISION: the three class names are RESERVED VALUES in the EXISTING model-string space. No schema\nchange; the DSL stays closed and its VALUE space gains three reserved words.\n\n    model: standard                                  # was: model: claude-sonnet-4-6\n    model_ladder: [trivial, standard, frontier]      # was: three literal ids\n\nResolution happens at the ONE funnel every step model already passes through —\nconfig.resolve_model(cfg, step=, workflow=) at config.py:347, today literally\n'return step or workflow or cfg.model'. If the resolved string is one of trivial/standard/frontier,\nresolve it through model_classes.load_class_slots(); otherwise return it unchanged. A literal id\nkeeps working byte-for-byte, so every existing workflow YAML is unaffected and the migration is\nopt-in per step.\n\nWHY NOT a new 'class:' key: it would fork the step schema (which runs.py documents as closed), force\nevery consumer to handle two mutually exclusive keys, and make 'model: standard' vs 'class: standard'\na silent ambiguity. Reserved values keep ONE resolution path.\n\nWHY NOT resolve at YAML load time: the class -> id mapping depends on env/CLI layers that are\nprocess-scoped, so a load-time resolution would freeze the wrong value in a long-lived process and\nwould not be visible to provider_provenance (343b).\n\nCOLLATERAL, and 7761's reviewer is right to flag it: sizing.MODEL_LADDER (sizing.py:99-103) hardcodes\nthe three BARE Anthropic names with their context windows, and models_at_or_above() returns those\nnames for runtime escalation. Its lookup is a SUBSTRING match ('if n in model'), so it happens to\nmatch a Bedrock id like bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6 — but it RETURNS the bare Anthropic\nname, so a Bedrock run that escalates would silently jump to DIRECT ANTHROPIC opus. That is a second\nsplit-brain, distinct from the entry-rung one 7761 already names, and it must be fixed in the same\nchange or the cutover leaks cross-provider on every escalation.\n\nSCOPE NOTE: this is specifying an unspecified mechanism, not redesigning the approved model. The\nthree-class model, the per-class provider/model/endpoint, the fallback chains and the layering\npointer are unchanged."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785495901282694001,"uuid":"7dcbb091-dcff-46b0-999f-96855b47bad3"}